# Expectation record for 04_bitfields/001_layout_and_size.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 001_layout_and_size
area               = 04_bitfields
description        = Bitfield sizeof, _Alignof and observable byte image, read through an unsigned char union member
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
The default undefined-behaviour warning gate applies unmodified, with no deviation, which is
why no ub_audit_flags key appears above.  That is measured rather than claimed: the program
compiles with zero diagnostics under the full gate -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion
-Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror, and it builds and runs cleanly under
-fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all, producing the golden stdout below and
writing nothing to standard error.

Object-representation determinacy is the argument this program rests on, because a byte image
is the one thing it prints that could be indeterminate.  A store into a bitfield leaves the
bits of its allocation unit that belong to no named field UNSPECIFIED afterwards, so zeroing
an overlay first buys freedom from reading uninitialized storage and nothing more.  A byte
image is therefore printed only for the two structs whose every bit belongs to a named field
-- oneword, whose two 16-bit fields cover a 32-bit unit exactly, and covered32, whose
1 + 14 + 7 + 10 fields partition one exactly.  The struct that necessarily leaves padding,
packed3, names 17 of its 32 bits and is observed only through sizeof, _Alignof and
named-member read-back; withsigned and spanning likewise.  Full bit coverage, not the zero
fill, is what makes an image determinate.

The byte image is read exclusively through an unsigned char array member of a union whose
other member is the bitfield struct, which is the standard's own route to an object
representation and the one route that cannot violate an aliasing rule: a character type may
alias any object.  No incompatible pointer type is formed or dereferenced anywhere in the
program, and no value is punned back out of the bytes -- each image is printed as bytes and
each field value is read from the field.

Nothing uninitialized is read.  The two automatic overlays are byte-written in full through
their unsigned char member, by a loop bounded by the sizeof of that member, before any byte
of either is read; the three volatile objects have static storage duration and therefore
begin zeroed.

Every value stored into a bitfield is representable in that field's declared width and
signedness, so no store is out of range and no conversion can change a value: the constant
variant stores literals that fit, and the runtime variant masks each volatile source to its
destination field's exact width before storing it.  No arithmetic is performed on any
bitfield.  The only operators the program executes are the pre-increment of an unsigned loop
counter bounded by the sizeof of a four- or eight-byte array, and bitwise AND masks against
unsigned constants, so there is no signed overflow and no operand can wrap.  No shift
operator appears in executable code at all, so no shift count can be out of range.

No object is modified twice between sequence points, no call takes an argument with a side
effect, and nothing depends on padding bytes or on the relative addresses of unrelated
objects.  Output is deterministic by construction: no address, pointer value, timestamp or
random value is printed, no locale-dependent conversion is used, the only conversion
specifiers are %u and %02x and each is given an operand cast to unsigned, and every loop
bound is the sizeof of a fixed-size array, so both iteration order and iteration count are
fixed.

Every read-back and every byte image is produced twice, once from constant initializers and
literals that a constant folder can evaluate whole and once through volatile-qualified
destinations fed from volatile sources, so the folder's answer cannot stand in for the one
the backend computes at run time.  The ten sizeof and _Alignof facts are compile-time
constants by nature and are printed once.

No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Every bitfield is declared on an explicit unsigned int or signed int base type.  A bitfield
declared on unqualified int has implementation-defined signedness, so using it would
manufacture a divergence that says nothing about code generation; stating the signedness in
the declaration removes that dependency outright.  The explicit int base type also fixes the
allocation unit at four bytes on every target under test.

Plain char is never used as a value type.  The byte image is an array of unsigned char, whose
signedness the standard fixes, which matters because plain char signedness was measured to
differ across the four targets -- signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64.

No value whose width varies by target is printed.  The documented target table records that
the pointer and long widths are four bytes on i686 and eight on the other three, so neither
sizeof(long) nor sizeof(void *) is printed and no address reaches the output.  Every printed
size and alignment belongs to a struct whose every field carries an int base type, and int is
four bytes under both the ILP32 model i686 uses and the LP64 model the other three use, so
all ten layout values are target-invariant by derivation rather than by coincidence.

How much storage a bitfield sequence is allocated, whether a field may straddle an allocation
unit boundary, and in what order fields are placed inside a unit are implementation-defined,
and the byte images are exactly what observes them.  The same table records all four targets
as little-endian, and the twelve configurations of this program -- four targets at each of
three optimization levels -- were measured to produce byte-identical output.  The program is
therefore compared on all four targets rather than restricted, and all three oracles stay
enabled; the measured agreement is the reason.  A cross-backend divergence here is a genuine
finding, not an implementation-defined difference, which is why no expected-divergence marker
is attached: the repository documents no bitfield limitation that could serve as a basis for
one.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
sizeof_packed3=4
alignof_packed3=4
sizeof_oneword=4
alignof_oneword=4
sizeof_withsigned=4
alignof_withsigned=4
sizeof_spanning=8
alignof_spanning=4
sizeof_covered32=4
alignof_covered32=4
packed3_readback a=5 b=21 c=300
oneword_readback lo=4660 hi=22136
oneword_image 34 12 78 56
covered32_readback a=1 b=12345 c=101 d=678
covered32_image 73 e0 b2 a9
volatile_packed3_readback a=5 b=21 c=300
volatile_oneword_readback lo=4660 hi=22136
volatile_oneword_image 34 12 78 56
volatile_covered32_readback a=1 b=12345 c=101 d=678
volatile_covered32_image 73 e0 b2 a9
END
